    year = "1994",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: This edited collection brings together historians and philosophers to assess, essay by essay, how Foucault's histories of madness, sexuality, and the human sciences hold up against the evidentiary and interpretive norms of the writing of history, with contributions ranging from sympathetic methodological readings to targeted empirical objections (including Megill's essay on the "rhetoric of historiography"). RELEVANCE: One of the earliest dedicated volumes to stage the disciplinary encounter this domain investigates directly - historians deliberately convened to evaluate Foucault as a historian - making it a central reference point for the appropriation/rejection debate and for tracking which specific empirical claims (confinement, sexuality, the "author function") drew historian scrutiny. POSITION: Mixed/edited volume spanning the full range of positions from empirical rejection to rhetorical reclassification and selective appropriation; not itself a single position but a structured forum for the debate.},
    keywords = "edited-collection, historiography, reception, method, Medium, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{stillvelody1992rewriting,
    editor = "Still, Arthur and Velody, Irving",
